      "problem": "If $A=[\\begin{aligned}4 & -7 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 5 & -3 & 4\\end{aligned}],$ then ${A}^{-1}=$",
      "worked_solution": "Let's find the characteristic equation $\\text{det}(A-\\lambda I)=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{det}(A-\\lambda I) & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}4-\\lambda  & -7 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1-\\lambda  & 0 \\\\ 5 & -3 & 4-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (1-\\lambda )|\\begin{aligned}4-\\lambda  & 3 \\\\ 5 & 4-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (1-\\lambda )((4-\\lambda )^{2}-15) & =0 \\\\ (1-\\lambda )({\\lambda }^{2}-8\\lambda +1) & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{3}-9{\\lambda }^{2}+9\\lambda -1 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the Cayley-Hamilton theorem, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{3}-9{A}^{2}+9A-I & =O.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $A$ is invertible, we can multiply this equation by ${A}^{-1}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{-1}({A}^{3}-9{A}^{2}+9A-I) & =O \\\\ {A}^{2}-9A+9I-{A}^{-1} & =O \\\\ {A}^{-1} & =9I-9A+{A}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$",
